---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/scorpion/', label: 'Play Scorpion' }}
>
  <p>
    Scorpion is a digging game. You build long suited runs right in the tableau, but the catch is
    that the cards you need are usually buried — and Scorpion's signature move lets you haul whole
    stacks around to free them. It rewards patience and a bit of nerve. This page is a complete
    reference to how Scorpion is played: the deal, the goal, the moves, and how you win. If you'd
    rather learn by doing, you can <a href="/scorpion/">play Scorpion</a> right away with undo and
    hints to back you up.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Scorpion Solitaire?</h2>
  <p>
    Scorpion is a solitaire game played with a single standard 52-card deck. It is a close cousin of
    <strong>Spider</strong>: you build sequences <strong>down in suit</strong> and aim to assemble
    full King-to-Ace runs. What sets Scorpion apart is the move rule — you may pick up
    <em>any</em> face-up card along with everything on top of it, in whatever order, and shift the
    whole group at once. There are no separate foundations to play to; the winning runs are built
    in place, right in the tableau.
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    Scorpion lays out <strong>seven tableau columns of seven cards each</strong> — 49 cards in all.
    The deal is partly hidden: in <strong>columns one to four</strong>, the bottom three cards of
    each column are dealt <strong>face down</strong>; <strong>columns five to seven</strong> are
    dealt <strong>entirely face up</strong>. The <strong>three remaining cards</strong> form a small
    <strong>face-down stock</strong> set to one side. So you start with a mix of visible and hidden
    cards, and part of the puzzle is exposing what you can't yet see.
  </p>

  <h2>The objective</h2>
  <p>
    You win by arranging all the cards into <strong>four suited sequences</strong>, each running
    from <strong>King down to Ace</strong> — one complete run per suit. These runs are built
    <strong>within the tableau</strong>; there is no separate pile to move them to. When all four
    same-suit King-to-Ace runs are assembled, the game is won.
  </p>

  <h2>Building down in suit</h2>
  <p>
    Scorpion builds <strong>down in suit</strong>. You may place a card on another card only if it
    is the <strong>same suit</strong> and <strong>exactly one rank higher</strong>. A 9&spades;
    goes onto a 10&spades;, a Queen&hearts; onto a King&hearts;, and so on. Colour and the other
    suits don't help here — it is strictly suit-matched. Over the game these matched cards link up
    into the long descending runs you need to win.
  </p>

  <h2>Moving cards — the Scorpion twist</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the rule that gives Scorpion its sting. You may pick up <strong>any face-up card
    together with every card stacked on top of it</strong> — even if that group is a jumbled,
    out-of-order mess — and move the whole pile onto a same-suit card one rank higher. The cards on
    top don't have to be in sequence; only the bottom card of the group you lift has to fit its new
    home.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, suppose a 7&clubs; you need is buried under a 2&hearts; and a J&spades;. If there
    is an 8&clubs; available elsewhere, you can lift the 7&clubs; <em>with</em> the 2&hearts; and
    J&spades; riding on top and drop the entire stack onto the 8&clubs;. The 7&clubs; is now placed
    correctly, and you can deal with the two passengers later. This is how you dig out cards that
    would be stuck for good in stricter games — you simply relocate whatever is sitting on them.
  </p>

  <h2>The three-card stock</h2>
  <p>
    The three leftover cards are your <strong>stock</strong>, and you get to use them
    <strong>once</strong>. When you deal the stock, the three cards go out face up, one onto each of
    the <strong>first three columns</strong>. There is <strong>no recycling</strong> — this deal
    happens a single time and never again. Because it is one-shot, time it well: deal the stock when
    those three fresh cards are likely to open up moves rather than bury more cards you need.
  </p>

  <h2>Empty columns</h2>
  <p>
    Clearing a column gives you room to manoeuvre, but an <strong>empty column can only be filled by
    a King</strong> (and any cards riding on top of it). Nothing else may start a fresh column. That
    makes empty columns valuable and a little awkward: hold one open for a King with a useful run
    already attached, rather than emptying a column when you have no King ready to move in.
  </p>
  <p>
    One handy side effect of all this shuffling: whenever you move the cards sitting above a
    <strong>face-down card</strong>, that card <strong>flips face up</strong>. Exposing hidden cards
    is most of the work in Scorpion, so look for moves that turn cards over even when they don't
    immediately extend a run.
  </p>

  <h2>How you win</h2>
  <p>
    The game is won the moment the tableau holds <strong>four complete suited runs</strong>, each
    from King down to Ace. Since the runs are built in place, there is no final flurry of moves to a
    foundation — once the last four King-to-Ace sequences are assembled, you're done. Scorpion is a
    tougher, more tactical relative of Spider, so expect to lean on undo while you learn which
    columns to dig into first.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Keep going</h2>
  <p>
    Now that you know the rules, <a href="/scorpion/">play a hand of Scorpion</a> and try the
    move-any-group trick for yourself. When you want a related challenge, try
    <a href="/spider/">Spider</a> for its in-order suited runs or <a href="/yukon/">Yukon</a> for
    another game that lets you move buried groups, and browse everything in our
    <a href="/guides/">guides hub</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
